Yes. Looking at the full sequence, the problem is not that information is missing; it’s that product data, protocol selection, dose calculation, reconstitution, administration, procurement, and QC are all competing at the same visual level.

I would reorganize it around one simple mental model:

Understand the peptide → Choose a protocol → Configure treatment → Prepare/administer → Verify quality

The biggest change I’d make is to stop showing the protocol simulator as another huge section inside the monograph. Once the physician selects a protocol, Atlas should switch into a focused protocol workspace, while the product monograph remains available as reference. Also, values such as strength, concentration, BAC volume, dose, units, vial yield, and storage should each have one authoritative location and be referenced elsewhere rather than repeatedly displayed.

One important correction: I would also remove a global “FDA Approved” badge from PT-141 unless the exact product/presentation shown is itself FDA-approved. The page can accurately say that bremelanotide has an FDA-approved reference product (Vyleesi), but that is different from implying that the displayed compounded/research vial is FDA-approved.

